{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00427 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Triprolidine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways, primarily through the indication of Nasopharyngitis and Tramazoline. The evidence suggests that Triprolidine may act as a precursor in the treatment of Nasopharyngitis, which in turn could influence the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis. This connection is supported by the shared indication of Nasopharyngitis in both Triprolidine and Tramazoline, which both lead to Vasomotor Rhinitis. While the exact mechanism by which Triprolidine contributes to the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis remains unclear, the evidence points to a potential role in the inflammatory or neurogenic pathways associated with nasal congestion and vasomotor instability. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering intermediate conditions in the context of drug repurposing and suggests that Triprolidine may have a broader therapeutic role in managing vasomotor symptoms.",
